EXT. TRAIN WATER TOWER, RAILWAY SIDING - NIGHT

A wooden tower on iron legs. A pump house. Rails vanish into dark prairie.

Two women sit under it, a small fire between them, boots stretched toward the heat.

WIDOW HESTER GALLOWAY-BIRD (60s, wide-brimmed hat, shotgun across her knees) pokes the embers with a stick.

TRAVELLING DOCTOR AMARA OKONKWO-WESTBROOK (40s, black bag, dusty skirt) writes in a leather book.

The fire cracks.

HESTER
You writing about me?

AMARA
About the weather.

HESTER
No weather in that book.

AMARA
It's a long book.

Hester snorts. Amara closes the cover, folds her hands.

HESTER
Where you headed?

AMARA
North. There's a fever in Redwater.

HESTER
Redwater's dead.

AMARA
Not all of it.

HESTER
Not all, I'll grant you.

AMARA
How far is it?

HESTER
Two days on foot. One on a horse.

AMARA
I've no horse.

HESTER
I have.

AMARA
Is she for sale?

HESTER
She's not a she. She's a gelding. And no.

AMARA
Rent, then.

HESTER
(to the fire)
I've been waiting thirty years for someone to ask me that.

AMARA
Is that a yes?

HESTER
It's a story.

AMARA
Tell it.

HESTER
My husband had a horse. Same gelding's mother. He rode her to Redwater the winter of the sickness. Carried medicine.

AMARA
And?

HESTER
He didn't come back.

AMARA
I'm sorry.

HESTER
Don't be. He was a fool. He said she'd carry him anywhere.

AMARA
Did she?

HESTER
She came back. Alone. Saddle empty.

AMARA
(softly)
She did her job.

HESTER
(to the fire)
Everybody does, eventually.

She stirs the embers. Sparks swirl into the black.

HESTER (CONT'D)
(louder, to the dark)
Hear that? She'll ride.

AMARA
Who are you talking to?

HESTER
The horse. And the husband.

Amara smiles in spite of herself. She reaches over, takes the stick, and pokes the fire herself.

AMARA
Then let's sleep. We leave at dawn.

HESTER
We?

AMARA
You're not letting a stranger ride your husband's horse alone.

Hester looks at her. For the first time, she laughs.

HESTER
Doctor, you're smarter than you look.

AMARA
I've been told.

They settle back. The rails hum faintly in the cold. Far off, a whistle.
